g the covering condition.  \n   Replacing exactly \\(k\\) of the lines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n3. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   When a line \\(L_{s}\\) is replaced by a sunny line \\(M\\) we choose the sunny direction so that \\(M\\) is not parallel to any of the remaining lines (the remaining \\(L_{t}\\) have slope \\(-1\\); the already introduced sunny lines have distinct slopes by construction). Hence all \\(n\\) lines stay distinct.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 we have a base configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \n   By repeatedly applying Lemma\u202f2 we obtain configurations for every \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   Therefore all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no other integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) consists of all lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (non\u2011sunny covering)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll the lines \\(L_{s}\\) are distinct because they have different constant terms.  \nEach \\(L_{s}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). By definition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge 2\\); therefore \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{s}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one)\n\nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be one of the lines from Lemma\u202f1 and let \\((a,b)\\in L_{s}\\cap T_{n}\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) is different from the slopes already used for sunny lines that have been introduced earlier.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nM_{s,\\alpha}:\\; y-b=\\alpha (x-a) .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(M_{s,\\alpha}\\) is a sunny line, it passes through \\((a,b)\\), and it is distinct from every line in the current family.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(M_{s,\\alpha}\\) is \\(\\alpha\\); by the choice of \\(\\alpha\\) it is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence \\(M_{s,\\alpha}\\) is sunny.  \n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(M_{s,\\alpha}\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) remains covered after the replacement.  \n\nAll remaining non\u2011sunny lines are still of the form \\(x+y=t\\) and therefore have slope \\(-1\\); the newly created line has slope \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), so it cannot coincide with any of them.  \nMoreover, by insisting that \\(\\alpha\\) be different from the slopes of previously created sunny lines, we guarantee that \\(M_{s,\\alpha}\\) does not coincide with any earlier sunny line. Hence all lines stay distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. Start with the family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{0}=\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\mathcal{F}_{0}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n2. For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) perform the following replacement:  \n   * pick the smallest index \\(s\\) such that \\(L_{s}\\in\\mathcal{F}_{i-1}\\);  \n   * choose any lattice point \\((a,b)\\in L_{s}\\cap T_{n}\\) (such a point exists because each line \\(L_{s}\\) contains the points \\((1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots\\));  \n   * select a slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) that is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and is different from \\(\\alpha_{1},\\d