y  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{0}=\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\mathcal{F}_{0}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n2. **Iterative replacement.**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) do:\n\n   * Choose the smallest index \\(s\\) such that \\(L_{s}\\in\\mathcal{F}_{i-1}\\).  \n   * Take the lattice point \\((a,b)=(1,s-1)\\in L_{s}\\cap T_{n}\\).  \n   * Select a slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) satisfying the conditions of Lemma 2 and different from \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{i-1}\\) (this is always possible because the set \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1,\\alpha_{1},\\dots ,\\alpha_{i-1}\\}\\) is infinite).  \n   * Replace \\(L_{s}\\) by the sunny line \\(M_{s,\\alpha_{i}}\\) defined in Lemma 2.\n\n   Denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal{F}_{i}\\).\n\n   By Lemma 2 each replacement keeps the covering of \\(T_{n}\\) intact and preserves distinctness of all lines. After the \\(i\\)-th step exactly \\(i\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Result.** After performing the above procedure \\(k\\) times we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and which still covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nA line is either sunny or not; therefore a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines. Consequently no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized. Negative values are not allowed by the statement of the problem. Hence the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe construction described above shows that every such \\(k\\) is attainable, and the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no other value can occur. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
