mple, take the even indices  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{i}=2i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n\n   which is possible because \\(2k\\le 2(n-1)\\le n+1\\) for \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n2. **Replace each selected line.**  \n   For each \\(s\\in S\\) do the following:  \n\n   - For every point \\((a,s-a)\\in P_{s}\\) introduce a new line  \n\n     \\[\n     M_{(a,s-a)}:\\; y-(s-a)=\\alpha_{(a,s-a)}\\bigl(x-a\\bigr),\n     \\]\n\n     where the slope \\(\\alpha_{(a,s-a)}\\) is chosen arbitrarily from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and distinct from all slopes already used.  \n\n   - Remove the original line \\(L_{s}\\) from the family.\n\n   Because the sets \\(P_{s}\\) are disjoint, the new lines introduced for different \\(s\\) never intersect at a required lattice point, and the distinctness of slopes guarantees that all new lines are distinct from each other and from the unchanged lines.\n\n3. **Count the lines.**  \n   The original family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) has \\(n\\) lines.  \n   For each selected \\(s\\) we delete one line and add \\(|P_{s}|\\) new lines.  \n   Since \\(|P_{s}|\\ge1\\) and the sets \\(P_{s}\\) are disjoint, the total number of lines after all replacements is  \n\n   \\[\n   n-\\lvert S\\rvert+\\sum_{s\\in S}|P_{s}|=n,\n   \\]\n\n   because each deleted line is compensated by exactly the same number of added lines (the points of \\(P_{s}\\) were previously covered only by that line).  \n\n   Moreover, every added line is sunny, while the untouched lines remain non‑sunny. Hence the final configuration contains exactly \\(\\displaystyle k=\\lvert S\\rvert\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Verification of covering.**  \n   - Points belonging to a replaced set \\(P_{s}\\) are each on a newly added sunny line by construction.  \n   - Points belonging to any other \\(P_{t}\\) with \\(t\\notin S\\) are still on the unchanged line \\(L_{t}\\).  \n   Therefore every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of the final family.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k