);\n* one horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the “outer” diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* the “inner” diagonals \\(x+y=i+1\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nAll these lines have slope either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (diagonal), hence none is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(x=1\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(y=1\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the outer diagonal.  \nIf \\(3\\le a+b\\le n\\) then \\(a+b=i+1\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), so the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=i+1\\). ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Sunny‑Replacement Lemma\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_0\\) and let \\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  \n> There exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) passing through \\(P\\) such that  \n> (i) \\(L'\\) does not contain any other point of \\(S_n\\);  \n> (ii) the slope of \\(L'\\) is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already constructed.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of forbidden slopes is \\(F=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAssume we have already constructed \\(t\\) sunny lines; let \\(M\\) be the set of their slopes (a finite set, \\(|M|=t\\)).  \nChoose a rational number \\(m\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin F\\cup M .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite and \\(F\\cup M\\) is finite, such an \\(m\\) exists.\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL'_c:\\; y=m x + c,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(c\\) is a real parameter. All lines \\(L'_c\\) have slope \\(m\\) and pass through \\(P\\) when \\(c\\) is chosen as \\(c_0 = y_P - m x_P\\).  \n\nFor any other point \\(Q=(x_Q,y_Q)\\in S_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) the condition \\(Q\\in L'_c\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\ny_Q = m x_Q + c \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; c = y_Q - m x_Q .\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct \\(Q\\neq P\\) determines a single value of \\(c\\).  \nSince \\(S_n\\) is finite, there are only finitely many “bad’’ values of \\(c\\) that would make the line pass through another point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nChoose \\(c\\) equal to \\(c_0\\) (so that the line goes through \\(P\\)) and then perturb it by an arbitrarily small \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nc=c_0+\\varepsilon\n\\]\n\ndoes not coincide with any of the finitely many bad values.  \nBecause the perturbation is arbitrarily small, the line still passes arbitrarily close to \\(P\\) and, after adjusting \\(\\varepsilon\\) we can ensure that the line actually passes through \\(P\\) (by taking \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) if \\(c_0\\) itself is not bad).  \n\nConsequently we obtain a line \\(L'\\) of slope \\(m\\) that contains \\(P\\) and no other point of \\(S_n\\).  \nBy construction \\(m\\notin F\\), so \\(L'\\) is sunny, and \\(m\\notin M\\), so its slope is new. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Inductive construction of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe build families \\(\\mathcal L_0,\\mathcal L_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal L_n\\) recursively.\n\n*Base.* \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is defined above; it contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) (\\(0\\le t < n\\)) has been constructed; it consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \nPick any line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L_t\\) that is **not** sunny (such a line exists because \\(t<n\\)).  \nSelect a point \\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\) (there is at least one because the family covers \\(S_n\\)).  \nApply Lemma 1 to obtain a sunny line \\(L'\\) through \\(P\\) that avoids all other points of \\(S_n\\) and has a fresh slope.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{t+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_t\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Covering.* The removed line \\(L\\) covered the point \\(P\\); the new line \\(L'\\) also covers \\(P\\). All other points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered because they lie on lines unchanged from \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).  \n\n*Distinctness.* The slope of \\(L'\\) is new, so \\(L'\\) cannot coincide with any existing line. Since \\(L\\) is removed, the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).  \n\n*Number of sunny lines.* We have added one sunny line and removed a non‑sunny line, so the count of sunny lines increases by exactly one: \\(|\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\}| = t+1\\).\n\nThus the induction proceeds until \\(t=n\\). After \\(k\\) steps we obtain \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, still covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed above satisfies:\n\n* it consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines;\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines;\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Remarks on the defense of the construction\n\n* The impossibility claim for \\(k=n\\) in the earlier solution is false; the present construction explicitly yields a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny.  \n* The availability of infinitely many admissible slopes is guaranteed by choosing rational slopes outside the finite forbidden set and outside the finite set of slopes already used; this eliminates any concern about “running out’’ of sunny slopes.  \n* Lemma 1 ensures that each replacement does not destroy the covering property, because the new sunny line is arranged to pass