iagonal \\(L_{a+b-1}\\) because \\((a-1,b+1)\\in S_{n}\\) and both diagonals intersect at \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) the point lies on \\(V\\) (resp. \\(H\\)) and also on the diagonal \\(L_{1+b}\\) (resp. \\(L_{a+1}\\)). ∎\n\nThus before any replacement each point of \\(S_{n}\\) is protected by a second line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Sunny‑Replacement Lemma (rigorous)\n\n> **Lemma 1 (Sunny‑Replacement).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal L^{\\!*}\\) be a family of distinct lines that still covers \\(S_{n}\\).  \n> Choose a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F\\) that is not sunny and a point \\(P\\in L\\cap S_{n}\\).  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(L'\\) such that  \n> 1. \\(P\\in L'\\) and \\(L'\\cap S_{n}=\\{P\\}\\);  \n> 2. the slope of \\(L'\\) is different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal F\\);  \n> 3. \\(L'\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(Q\\in S_{n}\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) define the slope that would make a line through \\(P\\) also pass through \\(Q\\):\n\n\\[\nm_{Q}= \\frac{y_{Q}-y_{P}}{x_{Q}-x_{P}} .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nF:=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{Q}\\mid Q\\neq P\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slopes of sunny lines already in }\\mathcal F\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  Choose a rational number \\(m\\notin F\\) (possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite).  The line\n\n\\[\nL':\\; y-y_{P}=m\\,(x-x_{P})\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(m\\), passes through \\(P\\), and by construction cannot pass through any other point of \\(S_{n}\\).  Since \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) the line is sunny, and because \\(m\\) is new it cannot coincide with any existing line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe build families \\(\\mathcal F_{0},\\mathcal F_{1},\\dots ,\\mathcal F_{k}\\) as follows.\n\n*Base.* \\(\\mathcal F_{0}:=\\mathcal L^{\\!*}\\).  It covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) has been constructed; it consists of \\(n+2\\) distinct lines, covers \\(S_{n}\\), and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause of Lemma 2, every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\); consequently there exists a non‑sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F_{t}\\) that still contains at least one point \\(P\\in S_{n}\\) (otherwise all non‑sunny lines would be empty, contradicting the existence of at least one non‑sunny line when \\(t<n-1\\)).  \n\nApply Lemma 1 to \\(L\\) and \\(P\\) and obtain a sunny line \\(L'\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{t+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal F_{t}\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Coverage.* The removed line \\(L\\) still has at least one other line of \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) covering each of its points, because before removal every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lay on at least two lines.  The distinguished point \\(P\\) is now covered by the newly inserted sunny line \\(L'\\).  Hence all points of \\(S_{n}\\) remain covered.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \\(L'\\) has a slope not used before, so it cannot coincide with any remaining line.  The total number of lines stays \\(n+2\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  One non‑sunny line is replaced by a sunny line, so the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one: \\(|\\{\\text{sunny in }\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\}|=t+1\\).\n\nThus the induction proceeds.  After \\(k\\) steps we have a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, still consisting of \\(n+2\\) distinct lines and covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Removing the auxiliary lines\n\nThe two auxiliary lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) belong to the original family \\(\\mathcal L^{\\!*}\\).  During the first two replacements they may be the lines that are removed; after