ns:\nhorizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and the direction \\((1,-1)\\) of the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A universal upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write it in the form \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\cap L\\) then \\(b=pa+q\\).  Because \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(p\\) must be rational; write \\(p=\\frac{u}{v}\\) with coprime integers \\(v>0\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{u}{v}a+q\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v b = u a + v q .\n\\]\n\nHence the integer points of \\(L\\) lying in the strip \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) form an arithmetic progression with step \\((v,u)\\).  Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\npoints; indeed the two extreme points of the progression differ by at least \\((1,1)\\) in both coordinates, so after at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) steps the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is violated.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }S_n\\text{ in at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points.}}\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  In each case the line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the whole column, the whole row, or the whole anti‑diagonal).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Necessary condition for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds above we obtain\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) and solve for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(M=m\\) and (2.4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nkm+(n-k)n\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow 2m-k\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=m+1\\) and (2.4) yields  \n\n\\[\nk(m+1)+(n-k)n\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus the numbers listed in the statement are **necessary**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Families of lines that will be used  \n\nWe introduce three families of non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V&=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\,\\}\\qquad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal H&=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\,\\}\\qquad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D&=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\,\\}\\qquad\\text{(anti‑diagonal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nEach of the three families alone covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor sunny lines we use the family of diagonals of slope \\(+1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_d=\\{\\,x-y=d\\mid d\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ |d|\\le n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nA line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_d\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in  \n\n\\[\n\\#(\\ell\\cap S_n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the lines with \\(|d|\\le n-2\\) contain exactly  \n\n\\[\nM=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\npoints; they are the *maximal* sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Construction for even \\(n=2m\\)\n\n#### 2.5.1  The “balanced’’ configuration (\\(k=m\\))\n\nTake the \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\;:\\;x-y=-(m-1)+2(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nFor each of them \\(|d|=m-1,m-3,\\dots ,m-1\\); by (2.9) each contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nConsequently the \\(m\\) lines cover \\(m^{2}\\) points.\n\nThe points that are not covered lie precisely in the columns \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,m\\); each of those columns contains \\(m+1,m,\\dots ,2\\) points, altogether \\(m(m+1)\\) points.  They are covered by the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\nThus we have a set of \\(n=2m\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k=m=n/2\\) of which are sunny.\n\n#### 2.5.2  Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\)\n\nIf we replace any of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which already belongs to the family (2.12)), the covering property is preserved because the column \\(x=i\\) was already completely covered by the vertical line.  Each such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one.  Repeating the operation we obtain configurations with any prescribed  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5.3  Obtaining any larger \\(k\\) (up to \\(n-1\\))\n\nStart from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (which uses \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines).  Replace, one after another, a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) of (2.11) that meets the same column \\(x=i\\) (choose \\(i=j\\)).  The new sunny line still covers all points of that column, and the points of the removed horizontal line are already covered by the remaining vertical lines of (2.12).  After \\(t\\) such replacements we have exactly \\(k=m+t\\) sunny lines.  Since we can perform at most \\(m-1\\) replacements (the last replacement would delete the final horizontal line and leave a column uncovered), we obtain all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{m,m+1,\\dots ,2m-1\\}= \\{\\,\\tfrac n2,\\tfrac n2+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2.13) and (2.14) we have realised **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Construction for odd \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\n#### 2.6.1  The “balanced’’ configuration (\\(k=m+1\\))\n\nTake the \\(m+1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\;:\\;x-y=-(m)+2(i-1)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m+1).\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\nEach of them satisfies \\(|d|\\le m\\) and therefore, by (2.9), contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the \\(m+1\\) lines cover \\((m+1)^{2}\\) points.\n\nThe uncovered points are those in the columns \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,m\\); their total number is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(m+1)^{2}=m(m+1).\n\\]\n\nThey are covered by the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\).  Altogether we have \\(n=2m+1\\) lines with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=m+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\]\n\nsunny members.\n\n#### 2.6.2  Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\)\n\nAs in the even case, replace any sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which already belongs to the covering set).  Each replacement lowers \\(k\\) by one, giving all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\}.\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.6.3  Obtaining any larger \\(k\\) (up to \\(n\\))\n\nWe start from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (again \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines).  As in the even case we replace a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) of (2.16) with \\(i=j\\).  After \\(t\\) replacements we have \\(k=\\frac{n+1}{2}+t\\) sunny lines.  Because there are \\(m\\) horizontal lines left after the first \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) replacements, we can perform at most \\(m\\) further replacements, reaching \\(k=n\\).\n\nThus we obtain all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2.17) this yields **every** integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  The full‑sunny construction for odd \\(n\\) (the case \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor completeness we give an explicit family of \\(n\\) sunny lines that already has \\(k=n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-2}{t-1}\\,x+\\frac{2}{t-1}.\n\\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the two points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\); when \\(t\\) is odd it also contains the middle point \\(\\bigl(\\frac{t-1}{2},\\frac{t-1}{2}\\bigr)