by the corresponding vertical line \\(x=i\\); the covering is unchanged and the number of sunny lines drops by one. Repeating we obtain every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\).\n\n   *Larger \\(k\\) (up to the bound \\(n-1\\)).*  \n   Start from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   Replace, one after another, a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{-(m-1)+2(j-1)}\\) (which meets exactly the same column).  \n   After \\(t\\) replacements we have \\(k=m+t\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because at most \\(m-1\\) horizontal lines can be removed without leaving a column uncovered, we obtain all integers  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{m,m+1,\\dots ,2m-1\\}= \\{\\,\\tfrac n2,\\tfrac n2+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Together with the smaller values we have realised **every** \\(k\\) satisfying (5) for even \\(n\\).\n\n   **Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).**  \n   *Balanced configuration* (exactly \\(k=m+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines).  \n   Take the \\(m+1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{-m},\\;\\ell_{-(m-2)},\\dots ,\\ell_{m}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   By (8) each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points, so they cover \\((m+1)^{2}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   The remaining \\(m(m+1)\\) points lie in the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) and are covered by the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\).  \n   Hence we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=m+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Smaller \\(k\\).*  \n   As before, replace any sunny line of (10) by the vertical line of the same column; each replacement reduces \\(k\\) by one. This yields all integers  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Larger \\(k\\) is impossible.*  \n   The bound (5) shows that for odd \\(n\\) we cannot have more than \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines; the construction above shows that this bound is sharp.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (5) is both necessary (by the capacity argument) and sufficient (by the explicit families).  \n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those stated in (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions\n\\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Maximal number of points on a sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \nLet \\((v,u)\\) be a primitive integer vector parallel to the line; then \\(p=u/v\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(u,v)=1\\) and \\(v>0\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on the line, then all integer points of the line are\n\n\\[\n(a+tv,\\;b+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nSince both coordinates increase by at least \\(1\\) when \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\),\nthe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can be satisfied for at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent values of \\(t\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }S_{n}\\text{ in at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points.}}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained only by the line \\(x-y=0\\) when \\(n\\) is odd; otherwise the\nlargest possible number is \\(M-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nOrder the sunny lines by the number of points they contain.\nFrom (2.2) the possible numbers are  \n\n\\[\nM,\\;M-1,\\;M-2,\\dots\n\\]\n\nand the line \\(x-y=d\\) contains \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_{k}=M+(M-1)+\\dots+(M-k+1)=kM-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nEach of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines is non‑sunny; such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \nTherefore a necessary condition for a covering is  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma_{k}+(n-k)n\\ge |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solve (2.4) for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(M=m\\) and (2.4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nkm+(n-k)n\\ge m(2m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=m+1\\) and (2.4) yields  \n\n\\[\nk(m+1)+(n-k)n\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\\Longrightarrow k\\le m+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n\\dfrac{n+1}{2},& n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Families of lines that will be used  \n\nVertical, horizontal and anti‑diagonal families (all non‑sunny):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nEach of \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) alone covers the whole set \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nSunny lines of slope \\(+1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d}:\\;x-y=d,\\qquad |d|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nFor such a line  \n\n\\[\n\\#(\\ell_{d}\\cap S_{n})=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(|d|\\le n-2\\) the right–hand side equals \\(M\\); the central line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) has\n\\(M\\) points if \\(n\\) is odd and \\(M-1\\) points if \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Constructions for even \\(n=2m\\)\n\n#### 2.4.1  Exactly \\(k=m=n/2\\) sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-(m-1)},\\;\\ell_{-(m-3)},\\dots ,\\ell_{m-1}.\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nEach contains \\(m\\) points, so together they cover \\(m^{2}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\nThe remaining \\(m(m+1)\\) points lie in the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) and are covered by the\n\\(m\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid1\\le i\\le m\\}\\).  \nThus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=m\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.4.2  Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\)\n\nReplace any of the sunny lines in (2.11) by the vertical line of the same column.\nThe covering is unchanged and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.\nRepeating we obtain all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4.3  Obtaining any larger \\(k\\) (up to the bound)\n\nStart from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by the sunny line\n\\(\\ell_{-(m-1)+2(j-1)}\\); this line covers exactly the same column that the removed\nhorizontal line helped to cover, while the points of that column are already taken care of by the vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid1\\le i\\le m\\}\\).  \nAfter \\(t\\) replacements we have \\(k=m+t\\) sunny lines.\nBecause at most \\(m-1\\) horizontal lines can be removed without leaving a column uncovered,\n\\(t\\) can range from \\(0\\) to \\(m-1\\), giving all  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{m,m+1,\\dots ,2m-1\\}= \\{\\,\\tfrac n2,\\tfrac n2+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2.12) we have realised every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Constructions for odd \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\n#### 2.5.1  Exactly \\(k=m+1=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(m+1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-m},\\;\\ell_{-(m-2)},\\dots ,\\ell_{m}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\n\nFor each of them \\(|d|\\le m\\), hence by (2.10) each contains \\(m+1\\) points.\nThus they cover \\((m+1)^{2}\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\nThe remaining \\(m(m+1)\\) points lie in the columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) and are covered by the\n\\(m\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid1\\le i\\le m\\}\\).  \nHence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=m+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.5.2  Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\)\n\nReplace any sunny line of (2.14) by the vertical line of the same column.\nEach replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering.\nThus we obtain all  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\}.\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.5.3  No configuration with more sunny lines\n\nInequality (2.6) shows that for odd \\(n\\) we cannot have more than \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) sunny lines.\nThe construction of §2.5.1 shows that this bound is attainable, so it is optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Final statement\n\nCombining the necessary bounds (2.7) with the explicit constructions of §§2.4 and 2.5 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is even:}& k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[6pt]\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd:}& k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) in the indicated set there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem, and for any integer outside the set such a family cannot exist. ∎"
